1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
She was born in France, though she is an American symbol.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:07,000
Like the immigrants she welcomed,

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:09,740
she was shunned by the rich and powerful.

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:12,740
She was underfunded, unappreciated,

5
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:15,040
and nearly undone by neglect.

6
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:18,580
Through it all, she has been an inspiration to millions.

7
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:21,620
Now, a story of hopes and dreams,

8
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:25,100
the Statue of Liberty on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The American colonies revolted in 1776,

10
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:41,540
the world recognized the rising tide of freedom.

11
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:45,440
Its intoxicating spirit traveled around the globe,

12
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:50,060
inspiring similar revolutions, especially in France,

13
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:55,060
where popular uprisings demanded the end of aristocratic rule.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
And so when the fragile American Union exploded into civil war

15
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:08,740
in 1861, the world held its breath.

16
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:11,540
Over a four-year period, the American landscape

17
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:15,380
was bathed in blood as over half a million men were slaughtered

18
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:17,520
on the fields of battle.

19
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:20,520
More than four times the US casualties in the Vietnam

20
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:22,520
and Korean wars combined.

21
00:01:22,521 --> 00:01:24,520
in the Vietnam and Korean wars combined.

22
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:31,760
Could a government of the people survive this brutal conflict,

23
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:34,540
which included the murder of its most beloved leader?

24
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:40,280
That America survived this historic crucible

25
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:42,900
became an inspiration around the world

26
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,280
and was felt most keenly in France,

27
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:49,240
where artists and intellectuals admired the American fortitude.

28
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:51,980
It was during an 1865 dinner party

29
00:01:51,981 --> 00:01:55,480
in the Paris home of Professor Edouard de la Boullée.

30
00:01:55,580 --> 00:01:58,720
But the idea was first suggested, create a monument

31
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:01,420
to honor America on its 100th anniversary

32
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:03,520
as the cradle of liberty.

33
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:06,060
It would be designed by one of the dinner guests,

34
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:09,600
Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi, to express the admiration

35
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:11,040
of the French people.

36
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:14,100
At the same time, it would serve as a public slap in the face

37
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,640
to Napoleon III, the reigning French monarch who had no taste

38
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:20,340
for the intellectual liberty of his own countrymen.

39
00:02:20,341 --> 00:02:23,540
Bartoli was an old hand at creating such monuments.

40
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:27,340
The arc of his career suggested that this female representation

41
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:30,280
of liberty would be a triumphant crescendo.

42
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:38,500
As an artist, he reflected the unique sensibilities of his age.

43
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:41,600
The period in which she was created,

44
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:43,900
and the second half of the last century,

45
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,340
was an enormously gigantic period of bravado

46
00:02:49,341 --> 00:02:54,640
and symbolism in the architecture of cities, in literature,

47
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:55,680
in art.

48
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:58,280
It was a period of great bursting out.

49
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:12,700
Bartolde's early sketches captured the work's

50
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:15,000
ultimate attitude and pose.

51
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:17,900
But it was the image of his mother's stern face which

52
00:03:17,901 --> 00:03:20,400
which supplied the enigmatic expression.

53
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:22,580
The final work would be called the Statue of Liberty

54
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,780
Enlightening the World, and its size

55
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:27,440
would mirror the mammoth proportions of the New World

56
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:28,780
itself.

57
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:31,520
It would stretch over 305 feet high.

58
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:35,520
The figure would be composed of 450,000 pounds

59
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:38,520
of hammered copper plates, enough metal

60
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:41,760
to press 30 million pennies.

61
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:47,360
The nose would be 4 1⁄2 feet long, each eye 2 1⁄2 feet wide.

62
00:03:47,361 --> 00:03:51,060
the face more than 17 feet from chin to brow.

63
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:54,460
Over 300 metal plates were needed to complete the figure,

64
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:57,040
designed to interlock in an artistic technique

65
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:58,800
called repoussé.

66
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:02,140
Monsieur Serge Pascal is one of the few remaining artisans

67
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:03,780
who still perform the technique.

68
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:12,180
The sculptor is going to work with a mass.

69
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:15,280
He's going to create his work from a block of stone.

70
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:20,860
Whereas we give a flat sheet a definite shape

71
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:23,660
that looks like something.

72
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:27,000
It was his company that recently worked with American crews

73
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:30,200
to restore the statue.

74
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:33,140
It begins by using a plombard pointing system

75
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:37,340
to form giant plaster replicas of the model design.

76
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:39,380
Then a wooden frame, a negative form,

77
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:41,480
is built around the plaster.

78
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:44,960
Into this frame, the copper sheets are formed.

79
00:04:44,961 --> 00:04:47,300
In the repoussage technique, we start out

80
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:49,260
with a flat sheet of metal.

81
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:50,760
It has to be shaped.

82
00:04:50,860 --> 00:04:54,700
It has to be what we call round embossing.

83
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:58,840
We had to introduce heat up to like 400 degrees Fahrenheit

84
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:03,820
to be able to get the forms to bend properly.

85
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,420
Hundreds of different types of hammers,

86
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:09,120
because each hammer had a certain amount of bounce to it,

87
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:11,760
a certain amount of pressure that it could perform,

88
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:14,460
a certain amount of pebbling effect that it would perform.

89
00:05:14,461 --> 00:05:20,920
We have inherited, if you will, this technique from our parents,

90
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:24,960
who with others helped make the Statue of Liberty in France.

91
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:31,580
It's both a tradition in the family as well as in the trade.

92
00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:34,100
Because the final structure is hollow,

93
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:38,760
keeping the statue's shell stable is an equally difficult engineering task.

94
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:44,040
Up to this time, the statues of this size are all solid.

95
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:46,600
And that was the original concept for this.

96
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:51,560
The original architect engineer that was working with Bartholdi

97
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:53,800
was Violette Leduc.

98
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:59,180
And he had a plan for filling the interior with boxes of sand

99
00:05:59,280 --> 00:06:01,820
to give it stability so the wind wouldn't tip it over

100
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:02,920
and so forth.

101
00:06:03,020 --> 00:06:04,820
In a divine act of providence,

102
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:06,680
Leduc suddenly died.

103
00:06:06,780 --> 00:06:10,120
And so did the notion of stuffing the statue with sand.

104
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:13,760
Instead, Gustave Eiffel, the engineering genius

105
00:06:13,761 --> 00:06:18,240
of the Eiffel Tower fame, proposed a radical new concept.

106
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:20,860
His concept, which is entirely new,

107
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:25,700
was to build a tower, an iron tower,

108
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:31,380
and then frame out from it and support the copper statue,

109
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:35,320
which was very much like a dressmaker's dummy.

110
00:06:35,420 --> 00:06:38,160
The result was a monument which, inside and out,

111
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:40,020
captured the imagination.

112
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:42,060
Its structural support was as radical

113
00:06:42,061 --> 00:06:46,160
is the new social structures defining the American ideal.

114
00:06:46,260 --> 00:06:49,100
Eiffel had mentioned that it was a magnificent space,

115
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:51,400
a cathedral kind of space inside.

116
00:06:56,200 --> 00:06:58,880
Bartholdi himself chose the site for his statue

117
00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:01,920
in America, a small patch of land in New York Harbor

118
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:03,620
called Bedloe Island.

119
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:05,920
It was an overgrown oyster bed named

120
00:07:06,020 --> 00:07:08,680
after an early Dutch settler who purchased the property

121
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:12,060
from the Mohican tribal nation.

122
00:07:12,061 --> 00:07:15,000
The United States had built a fort on the island in 1800,

123
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:17,560
and it served as the site for a military hospital,

124
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:21,400
communication center, and ammo dump during the Civil War.

125
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:24,000
Bartolde's vision to erect the statue here

126
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:27,080
was a bold move and a logistical challenge which

127
00:07:27,180 --> 00:07:29,540
would demand substantial funding.

128
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:33,780
So in 1874, he formed the Union Franco-American Committee

129
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:37,080
to raise the funds through public lotteries.

130
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:40,380
He had already decided to begin work on the copper repoussé

131
00:07:40,381 --> 00:07:42,940
before the money had actually been donated,

132
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:45,560
hoping that there would be sufficient contributions

133
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:47,320
to finish.

134
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:51,500
Bartholdi's Paris workshop was big and loud and packed

135
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:54,460
with an army of exacting artisans.

136
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:56,860
Even ex-president Ulysses S. Grant

137
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:59,440
dropped by to watch the progress.

138
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:02,400
But the work was slow and underfunded.

139
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:06,040
By 1876, the year the statue was to be completed,

140
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:10,380
only the right arm and torch were in shape for a presentation.

141
00:08:10,381 --> 00:08:12,820
They were sent to Philadelphia as part of the American

142
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:14,820
Centennial Exposition.

143
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:18,320
The hope was to build interest and generate contributions

144
00:08:18,420 --> 00:08:20,560
towards the completion of the gift.

145
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:23,260
By this time, Bartoli had decided that France

146
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:25,100
would supply the statue and America

147
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:28,240
should construct the pedestal, even though the United States

148
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:30,540
had yet to accept the colossal gift,

149
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:33,940
and Bartoli hadn't acquired the rights to use Bedloe's Island.

150
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:40,040
What a weird idea to give a huge statue.

151
00:08:40,041 --> 00:08:42,780
And there was a lot of pulling and pushing.

152
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:44,040
He said, no, we don't want it.

153
00:08:44,140 --> 00:08:49,680
And finally, the US ambassador had convinced the Congress

154
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:51,920
to say, no, you must take it because the French are going

155
00:08:52,020 --> 00:08:52,980
to be upset.

156
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:55,860
Many people were suspicious of the French gift celebrating

157
00:08:55,960 --> 00:08:57,560
the American Revolution.

158
00:08:57,660 --> 00:08:59,220
Would the sculpture portray liberty

159
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:04,560
as a bare-breasted revolutionary as rendered in European art?

160
00:09:04,660 --> 00:09:07,640
The New York Times suggested that the statue was a scam

161
00:09:07,641 --> 00:09:11,240
to make a personal fortune.

162
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:14,040
And American millionaires like J.P. Morgan

163
00:09:14,140 --> 00:09:16,880
and Cornelius Vanderbilt joined Grover Cleveland

164
00:09:16,980 --> 00:09:19,260
in declaring that the promotion of liberty

165
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:23,020
would cause unrest among America's lower class,

166
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:24,820
the working poor.

167
00:09:24,920 --> 00:09:28,100
But with the enthusiastic support of newspaper publisher

168
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:33,040
Joseph Pulitzer, the American committee was formed in 1877

169
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:37,000
to organize grassroots funding after the government

170
00:09:37,001 --> 00:09:40,940
the wealthy and their corporations turned their backs.

171
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:43,580
Well, every trick was used 100 years ago

172
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:46,640
to try to raise money from selling toothpaste,

173
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:48,780
which was the Statue of Liberty toothpaste,

174
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:51,420
to selling small replicas.

175
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:54,220
Even before she was fully assembled,

176
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:57,480
the Statue of Liberty became a celebrity spokesperson

177
00:09:57,580 --> 00:09:59,720
for all manner of products in order

178
00:09:59,820 --> 00:10:02,520
to raise construction funds.

179
00:10:02,620 --> 00:10:07,000
She has been an advertising icon ever since.

180
00:10:07,001 --> 00:10:10,840
The product endorsement feeding frenzy

181
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:14,500
took the new art of hucksterism to dizzying heights.

182
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:16,180
But it wasn't enough.

183
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:19,520
Pulitzer realized that the project had to tap into America's

184
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:23,480
soul, and he persuaded a young poetess, Emma Lazarus,

185
00:10:23,580 --> 00:10:28,260
to compose a stirring verse to capture the public's imagination.

186
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:31,320
The poem was called The New Colossus.

187
00:10:31,420 --> 00:10:35,300
It was inspired by the tragic slaughter of Russian Jews

188
00:10:35,301 --> 00:10:39,300
during the pogroms of the era.

189
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:43,300
Give me your tired, your poor, your huddled masses yearning

190
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:45,340
to breathe free, she wrote.

191
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:48,240
The wretched refuse of your teeming shore.

192
00:10:48,340 --> 00:10:53,460
Send these, the homeless, the tempest tossed to me.

193
00:10:53,560 --> 00:10:57,360
Meanwhile, liberty was erected on the streets of Paris,

194
00:10:57,460 --> 00:11:01,400
an exile herself, waiting for the money and the permission

195
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:05,300
to emigrate to the United States.

196
00:11:05,301 --> 00:11:08,140
Though many French citizens were determined that she not

197
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:10,900
be given to a seemingly ungrateful America,

198
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,940
the finished statue was presented to US Ambassador

199
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:19,240
Levi P. Morton on July 4, 1884.

200
00:11:19,340 --> 00:11:21,180
One month later in the United States,

201
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:24,520
work began on construction of the statue's gigantic pedestal,

202
00:11:24,620 --> 00:11:26,960
a 154-foot-high behemoth.

203
00:11:27,060 --> 00:11:29,360
Nearly as tall as the statue itself,

204
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:32,920
the foundation would be recessed 53 feet into the earth

205
00:11:32,921 --> 00:11:36,720
to support the massive weight and spread over more than 8,000

206
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:39,660
square feet at the base.

207
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:44,540
It was the largest placement of concrete, mass concrete,

208
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:46,960
that had been done up to that time.

209
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:51,500
And it was still in a state of development.

210
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:54,880
The cornerstone was a six-ton slab of Connecticut granite

211
00:11:54,980 --> 00:12:00,680
set in place on a rainy afternoon ceremony on August 5, 1884.

212
00:12:00,681 --> 00:12:02,820
Yet the celebration was short-lived.

213
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:06,380
Less than three weeks later, the project had exhausted its funds

214
00:12:06,480 --> 00:12:08,120
and work stopped.

215
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:12,620
So the statue was accepted, was built and ready to be shipped,

216
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:14,920
and there was still no pedestal because there

217
00:12:15,020 --> 00:12:19,140
was no money to really pay for this pedestal.

218
00:12:19,240 --> 00:12:21,440
Once again, Joseph Pulitzer attacked

219
00:12:21,540 --> 00:12:23,440
what he called the American disgrace

220
00:12:23,540 --> 00:12:26,140
by rallying the support of the American people,

221
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:28,800
offering children, the poor and the humble,

222
00:12:28,801 --> 00:12:31,400
an opportunity to donate their pennies and nickels

223
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:34,100
to the effort and, in exchange, see their names

224
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:37,000
printed on the front page of his newspaper.

225
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:39,540
The effort was a resounding success,

226
00:12:39,640 --> 00:12:43,740
raising $102,000 and helping the Liberty design

227
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:46,960
become stylish and fashionable.

228
00:12:47,060 --> 00:12:48,960
Cornelius Vanderbilt's daughter even

229
00:12:49,060 --> 00:12:52,400
dressed as Liberty for a festive social affair.

230
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:55,560
The party cost more than the building of the statue itself,

231
00:12:55,660 --> 00:12:58,000
and her upper-crust party guests refused

232
00:12:58,001 --> 00:13:00,700
to contribute to the celebration of the common man.

233
00:13:08,740 --> 00:13:12,580
June 17, 1885, the Statue of Liberty

234
00:13:12,680 --> 00:13:14,520
arrived in New York Harbor.

235
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:19,720
More than 300 pieces distributed in 214 numbered crates.

236
00:13:19,820 --> 00:13:22,320
It would take over four weeks before the crates

237
00:13:22,420 --> 00:13:24,000
were even unloaded.

238
00:13:24,100 --> 00:13:25,700
But there was no hurry.

239
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:27,400
Construction of the pedestal had yet

240
00:13:27,401 --> 00:13:29,700
had yet to be completed.

241
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:32,800
For 10 months, Liberty's assorted body parts

242
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:35,540
littered Bedloe's Island until the work of bonding

243
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:40,640
the enormous copper plates to the iron armature could begin.

244
00:13:40,740 --> 00:13:43,340
A spiraling staircase was attached to the center

245
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:45,820
structural beam, which allowed workmen access

246
00:13:45,920 --> 00:13:48,820
to the elevated points at which pieces would be secured

247
00:13:48,920 --> 00:13:50,360
to the frame.

248
00:13:50,460 --> 00:13:53,360
Though now closed, this same staircase

249
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:57,400
was once used by visitors to climb to the top of the monument

250
00:13:57,401 --> 00:14:01,200
through the hollow cathedral of Liberty's form.

251
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:05,600
The giant jigsaw puzzle didn't always fit as planned.

252
00:14:05,700 --> 00:14:08,040
Liberty's crown, seven rays of light,

253
00:14:08,140 --> 00:14:10,240
representing the seven continents,

254
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:13,380
was positioned too close to her right arm,

255
00:14:13,480 --> 00:14:15,760
allowing a dagger-like ray of light

256
00:14:15,860 --> 00:14:17,860
to actually pierce her copper flesh.

257
00:14:21,260 --> 00:14:24,460
That was what we referred to as a birth defect.

258
00:14:24,461 --> 00:14:29,300
That occurred while it was being constructed.

259
00:14:29,400 --> 00:14:32,660
It was the artist himself who was responsible for the flaw

260
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:35,500
back at the studio in Paris.

261
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:39,300
Bartoldi, the sculptor, asked for a modification,

262
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:43,220
which is, after all, the least that a sculptor doing a work

263
00:14:43,320 --> 00:14:46,920
of this size would be expected to ask.

264
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:50,520
The modification was to raise Liberty's arm and torch

265
00:14:50,620 --> 00:14:53,920
a few degrees higher than the original design.

266
00:14:53,921 --> 00:14:57,860
But this change compromised the statue's structural strength.

267
00:14:57,960 --> 00:15:01,960
Because it was moved, it became much less stiff,

268
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:05,740
much less strong, and took a lot of ingenuity

269
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:07,200
to make it work.

270
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:09,900
The defect often blamed on the American workers

271
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:11,640
who assembled the statue remained

272
00:15:11,740 --> 00:15:13,980
uncorrected for the next 100 years.

273
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:28,260
Finally, on October 28, 1886, the Statue of Liberty

274
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:29,060
was complete.

275
00:15:33,160 --> 00:15:37,800
10 years and 116 days after the original American centennial

276
00:15:37,900 --> 00:15:40,140
delivery date.

277
00:15:40,240 --> 00:15:44,380
After 21 years of planning, fundraising, and sculpting,

278
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:45,780
Liberty stood tall.

279
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:53,480
Across a fog shrouded harbor, nearly a million people

280
00:15:53,481 --> 00:15:56,520
struggled to view the unveiling ceremonies.

281
00:15:56,620 --> 00:15:59,060
Liberty appeared as a silhouette through the mist.

282
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:02,520
A tricolored French flag covered her face.

283
00:16:02,620 --> 00:16:05,320
President Grover Cleveland, who once opposed the statue,

284
00:16:05,420 --> 00:16:08,300
welcomed a crowd of dignitaries on the island.

285
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:10,440
Ironically, American women were not

286
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:12,460
allowed to participate in the festivities

287
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:15,740
honoring a feminine representation of liberty.

288
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:19,140
We cannot guarantee their safety among a crowd of men,

289
00:16:19,240 --> 00:16:21,180
officials claimed.

290
00:16:21,181 --> 00:16:24,020
Enraged members of the New York State Women's Suffragette

291
00:16:24,120 --> 00:16:27,680
Association chartered a boat and stormed the ceremony anyway,

292
00:16:27,780 --> 00:16:30,060
chanting their displeasure through megaphones

293
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:31,960
to disrupt the proceedings.

294
00:16:32,060 --> 00:16:34,900
The pandemonium they triggered cut short the long-winded

295
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,660
speeches when Liberty's veil was dropped prematurely.

296
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:44,940
The following day, professional orator Chauncey Depew

297
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:48,540
recreated his aborted speech for posterity

298
00:16:48,541 --> 00:16:53,540
On a new technological miracle, a clay cylinder recording device.

299
00:16:55,540 --> 00:16:59,540
Here is his actual voice, captured over a century ago.

300
00:16:59,541 --> 00:17:00,440
ago.

301
00:17:16,220 --> 00:17:20,960
At long last, Liberty had made her home in America,

302
00:17:21,060 --> 00:17:24,300
looking out to sea and beckoning many more immigrants

303
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:27,900
towards the golden door of the new world.

304
00:17:27,901 --> 00:17:30,200
And with their arrival, the nation's attention

305
00:17:30,300 --> 00:17:33,840
would shift to another small island in the harbor,

306
00:17:33,940 --> 00:17:34,840
Ellis Island.

307
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:52,900
Cavera, Guihou, Einstein, Garibaldi, McCormick, Nielson.

308
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,800
Form a single line.

309
00:17:55,801 --> 00:17:59,800
My name is Hermann Müller. I come from Hamburg, Germany.

310
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:29,640
in size to 27 and a half acres.

311
00:18:29,740 --> 00:18:32,640
Like Liberty Island, where the statue stands,

312
00:18:32,740 --> 00:18:37,940
Ellis Island was a forgotten patch of land.

313
00:18:38,040 --> 00:18:40,540
This mass immigration that was coming

314
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:44,820
was very financially beneficial to the steamship companies.

315
00:18:44,920 --> 00:18:48,360
They would go over, say, to a small village in Italy

316
00:18:48,460 --> 00:18:51,260
and put up posters of a cornucopia.

317
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:54,060
And the immigrants literally believed

318
00:18:54,061 --> 00:18:57,060
that there was gold everywhere.

319
00:18:57,061 --> 00:19:03,060
1920, my father aged about 33 and my mother was about 30.

320
00:19:03,061 --> 00:19:11,060
And my sister, who was five months old, and myself embarked for America.

321
00:19:11,061 --> 00:19:17,060
The Golden Medina, the land where the streets were paved with gold.

322
00:19:17,061 --> 00:19:31,061
We sailed out of Genoa in the month of January 1920, and the ship was always stopping on the way to pick up coal, and it took us 30 days to get here.

323
00:19:31,140 --> 00:19:38,171
The ship was crowded. It wasn't clean. We were frightened.

324
00:19:38,172 --> 00:19:46,777
The smell of bodies all over the place. It was difficult living aboard.

325
00:19:46,778 --> 00:19:49,080
And we were hungry.

326
00:19:49,081 --> 00:19:58,080
We were so hungry that I remember vividly that my father and I went up to the first

327
00:19:58,081 --> 00:20:04,080
deck and found the garbage cans on that deck.

328
00:20:07,080 --> 00:20:14,080
And the garbage cans, we dug down and brought up food.

329
00:20:14,081 --> 00:20:22,380
And we brought it down for my mother, and my mother would never eat it.

330
00:20:22,381 --> 00:20:28,600
The steamships would pull into Manhattan, and two medical inspectors from Ellis Island

331
00:20:28,601 --> 00:20:33,840
would board the steamships and go to the cabin class people and the first class people and

332
00:20:33,841 --> 00:20:38,680
give them a very quick medical examination aboard the steamship.

333
00:20:38,681 --> 00:20:42,440
They felt that if these people could afford the $65

334
00:20:42,441 --> 00:20:45,220
for an expensive ticket, they could obviously

335
00:20:45,221 --> 00:20:48,060
support themselves.

336
00:20:48,061 --> 00:20:50,460
The people that traveled to Ellis are those

337
00:20:50,461 --> 00:20:53,540
that traveled in steerage.

338
00:20:53,541 --> 00:20:56,140
While the rich were invited to walk right down

339
00:20:56,141 --> 00:20:59,880
the gangplank into Manhattan, the poor were not

340
00:20:59,881 --> 00:21:02,500
allowed off the boat.

341
00:21:02,501 --> 00:21:04,520
And traveling below deck in steerage,

342
00:21:04,521 --> 00:21:06,520
they never saw the Statue of Liberty

343
00:21:06,521 --> 00:21:08,760
welcoming them to America.

344
00:21:08,761 --> 00:21:11,920
Instead, they were herded onto crowded ferries

345
00:21:11,921 --> 00:21:14,900
and shipped to the imposing brick and tile processing

346
00:21:14,901 --> 00:21:17,480
center on Ellis Island, a collection

347
00:21:17,481 --> 00:21:21,240
of bureaucratic buildings where their American experience

348
00:21:21,241 --> 00:21:23,280
would begin.

349
00:21:23,281 --> 00:21:26,380
They would enter Ellis Island to the baggage room.

350
00:21:26,381 --> 00:21:29,360
They would have the opportunity to check their bags

351
00:21:29,361 --> 00:21:31,640
so they wouldn't have to carry them while they were being

352
00:21:31,641 --> 00:21:32,680
processed.

353
00:21:32,681 --> 00:21:35,720
Well, if you were a Russian Jew escaping the pogroms

354
00:21:35,721 --> 00:21:41,880
Russia a man in uniform would be very frightening to you if they were all the

355
00:21:41,881 --> 00:21:45,900
possessions you owned in the world you wouldn't want to give them up but there

356
00:21:45,901 --> 00:21:50,300
was actually an even more important reason why the immigrants held on to

357
00:21:50,301 --> 00:21:54,640
their baggage they knew that they were about to undergo a medical examination

358
00:21:54,641 --> 00:21:57,880
if they had something wrong with them they would not be allowed into the

359
00:21:57,881 --> 00:22:02,780
United States so supposing you had a bad leg you would want to walk and carry a

360
00:22:02,781 --> 00:22:07,580
bag in front of your bad leg and to pretend that it was the weight of the bag that was

361
00:22:07,581 --> 00:22:12,580
causing you to limp.

362
00:22:12,581 --> 00:22:17,660
From the baggage room, the immigrants climbed a very steep and narrow staircase and they

363
00:22:17,661 --> 00:22:23,600
didn't realize it, but as they were climbing, their medical examination had begun.

364
00:22:23,601 --> 00:22:28,540
There were two doctors standing at the top of the stairs with a piece of chalk and as

365
00:22:28,541 --> 00:22:30,880
As the immigrants climbed the stairs,

366
00:22:30,881 --> 00:22:34,640
the doctors were watching to see who had a limp.

367
00:22:34,641 --> 00:22:37,940
And if you were lame, you got an L on your lapel.

368
00:22:37,941 --> 00:22:40,120
If you were huffing and puffing, you might have a heart

369
00:22:40,121 --> 00:22:40,960
condition.

370
00:22:40,961 --> 00:22:41,661
You'd get an H.

371
00:22:45,420 --> 00:22:46,120
Back problems.

372
00:22:49,220 --> 00:22:52,560
Conjunctivitis.

373
00:22:52,561 --> 00:22:53,560
Goiter.

374
00:22:55,960 --> 00:22:58,540
Hernia.

375
00:22:58,541 --> 00:23:07,040
Pregnant, scalp infection, senility,

376
00:23:07,140 --> 00:23:09,920
suspected mental defect.

377
00:23:10,020 --> 00:23:12,280
The most dreaded examination of all

378
00:23:12,380 --> 00:23:15,960
was a doctor referred to as the eye man.

379
00:23:16,060 --> 00:23:19,320
And he was looking for a disease called trachoma.

380
00:23:19,420 --> 00:23:21,260
It was extremely contagious.

381
00:23:21,360 --> 00:23:22,900
It would lead to blindness.

382
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:24,460
And if you caught it, you would not

383
00:23:24,560 --> 00:23:26,860
be allowed into the United States.

384
00:23:26,861 --> 00:23:30,860
The inspector would stand there with a basin of antiseptic and an old-fashioned button

385
00:23:30,861 --> 00:23:35,480
hook and he would take the button hook, dip it in the antiseptic, flip it into your eyelid

386
00:23:35,481 --> 00:23:37,320
and flip your eyelid back.

387
00:23:37,321 --> 00:23:41,820
He was looking for any redness or inflammation and that was a sign of trachoma.

388
00:23:41,821 --> 00:23:48,740
You get a TC on your lapel and you would be put in quarantine until they found out what

389
00:23:48,741 --> 00:23:52,600
was wrong with you.

390
00:23:52,601 --> 00:24:00,900
My father received a chalk mark on his lapel, and not realizing what it meant, he didn't

391
00:24:00,901 --> 00:24:03,980
wipe it off like others did.

392
00:24:03,981 --> 00:24:11,060
It seems that while on ship, he shaved with a straight razor and cut his face and had

393
00:24:11,061 --> 00:24:13,180
a little scar.

394
00:24:13,181 --> 00:24:18,220
And so he was taken from the line for investigation.

395
00:24:18,221 --> 00:24:25,100
mother was frantic begging and pleading and even offered her marriage ban they should let him stay

396
00:24:25,101 --> 00:24:36,380
in the line but they took him out the main hall was designed like cattle stockyards to maximize

397
00:24:36,381 --> 00:24:43,820
the movement of human cargo he had two minutes to ask a series of 29 questions what's your name

398
00:24:43,821 --> 00:24:47,340
where did you come from why did you come here how much money do you have where did you get it let

399
00:24:47,341 --> 00:24:48,041
Let me see it.

400
00:24:48,042 --> 00:24:49,500
Who paid for your passage?

401
00:24:49,501 --> 00:24:53,220
Do you have any friends here, any relatives?

402
00:24:53,221 --> 00:24:57,120
Very often, I think of the enormity of the decision

403
00:24:57,121 --> 00:24:58,680
by the authorities.

404
00:24:58,681 --> 00:25:02,020
Had they decided to send me back,

405
00:25:02,021 --> 00:25:06,000
I probably would have been a bar of soap.

406
00:25:06,001 --> 00:25:08,580
Many European Jews denied admission

407
00:25:08,581 --> 00:25:13,160
eventually wound up on death lists back home.

408
00:25:13,161 --> 00:25:16,640
many rejected Asians were sent back to hardship and persecution.

409
00:25:17,620 --> 00:25:22,560
There were a group, an entire shipload of Italians coming through

410
00:25:22,561 --> 00:25:24,760
that did not have their papers.

411
00:25:25,720 --> 00:25:28,660
And their papers, their official papers,

412
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:35,000
were ultimately initial W period, O period, P period, without papers.

413
00:25:35,220 --> 00:25:40,340
So the workers at Ellis Island, just for shorthand, would say,

414
00:25:40,341 --> 00:25:45,080
oh, we have three wops here, and there are five wops over there.

415
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:47,040
If a person was a Christian, they

416
00:25:47,140 --> 00:25:51,020
would put a little crucifix on the top of the page.

417
00:25:51,120 --> 00:25:53,480
And if they were Jewish, they would put a circle.

418
00:25:53,580 --> 00:25:56,160
Well, the word for that is keichel.

419
00:25:56,260 --> 00:25:59,200
The new world was full of surprises.

420
00:25:59,300 --> 00:26:02,660
The first was food.

421
00:26:02,760 --> 00:26:05,660
The original food concessionaire stole most of the money

422
00:26:05,760 --> 00:26:07,840
he was given to feed the immigrants with,

423
00:26:07,841 --> 00:26:11,600
And he wound up serving them stewed prunes over dark bread twice a day.

424
00:26:12,480 --> 00:26:13,440
He was fired.

425
00:26:13,920 --> 00:26:16,540
And as time went on, with fewer immigrants coming,

426
00:26:16,900 --> 00:26:18,840
they were able to serve ethnic meals.

427
00:26:19,200 --> 00:26:24,140
They would have a German meal at 1 o'clock, an Italian meal at 2 o'clock, and so on.

428
00:26:24,141 --> 00:26:31,780
But that led to the problem of the Germans showing up at the Italian meal of spaghetti and tomato sauce,

429
00:26:31,880 --> 00:26:35,620
and a lot of them would refuse to eat it, saying it was just too frightening looking.

430
00:26:35,621 --> 00:26:40,620
We stayed for 10 days in the big hall.

431
00:26:40,621 --> 00:26:45,620
And they were double and triple bunk beds in there, very, very closed.

432
00:26:45,621 --> 00:26:50,620
And they were very, very crowded and very, very smelly and very, very noisy.

433
00:26:50,621 --> 00:26:53,620
It was constant turmoil.

434
00:26:53,621 --> 00:26:59,620
And each morning I would take the two mattresses to the heap,

435
00:26:59,621 --> 00:27:03,620
and each night I would bring them back to put on the bed.

436
00:27:03,621 --> 00:27:08,620
And I only learned recently that the purpose of the mattresses

437
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:12,100
being taken away was to fumigate them.

438
00:27:12,200 --> 00:27:14,100
The massive steam pressure tanks

439
00:27:14,200 --> 00:27:16,160
used to delouse mattresses are still

440
00:27:16,260 --> 00:27:20,340
in storage at Ellis Island, grim artifacts of a time

441
00:27:20,440 --> 00:27:24,180
when immigrants were considered as so many diseased rodents,

442
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:29,380
ready to invade polite American society.

443
00:27:29,480 --> 00:27:32,380
New immigrants often reminded established immigrants

444
00:27:32,381 --> 00:27:35,720
of a dismal past they longed to forget.

445
00:27:35,820 --> 00:27:39,220
These new arrivals were a threat to the status quo

446
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:42,560
and an embarrassment to be hidden away in overcrowded

447
00:27:42,660 --> 00:27:44,460
ghettos.

448
00:27:44,560 --> 00:27:48,300
Here, people would accept any job at any pay

449
00:27:48,400 --> 00:27:52,760
to make their American dream come true.

450
00:27:52,860 --> 00:27:55,640
But as the nation grew, the Ellis Island immigrants

451
00:27:55,740 --> 00:27:58,700
assimilated into communities coast to coast.

452
00:27:58,800 --> 00:28:01,380
Their legacy is the solid infrastructure

453
00:28:01,381 --> 00:28:07,160
of the country upon which rests the 20th century.

454
00:28:07,260 --> 00:28:10,620
Today, Ellis Island still celebrates the contributions

455
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:13,600
of those people whose American dream began there

456
00:28:13,700 --> 00:28:16,900
with a museum and a program which keep their stories alive.

457
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:19,040
Well, it was quite a day for me.

458
00:28:19,140 --> 00:28:23,040
Here, immigrants return to record their memories,

459
00:28:23,140 --> 00:28:26,480
offering the future a unique look at the past.

460
00:28:26,580 --> 00:28:27,880
It was a very overwhelming feeling, and I choke up now

461
00:28:27,980 --> 00:28:29,880
as I remember it.

462
00:28:29,881 --> 00:28:33,220
As twin national monuments, the Statue of Liberty

463
00:28:33,320 --> 00:28:36,920
and Ellis Island represent two points on the path

464
00:28:37,020 --> 00:28:41,320
to the American dream, where we've come from

465
00:28:41,420 --> 00:28:44,420
and where we're going to.

466
00:28:44,520 --> 00:28:46,700
And the real spirit of liberty lies

467
00:28:46,800 --> 00:28:51,400
in the delicate balance between these two glorious notions.

468
00:28:55,300 --> 00:28:57,700
By 1900, the sea, air, and elements

469
00:28:57,701 --> 00:29:00,800
had changed Liberty's complexion from bright copper orange

470
00:29:00,900 --> 00:29:04,740
to pale green, a fitting way to begin the 20th century,

471
00:29:04,840 --> 00:29:09,320
which was itself defined by change.

472
00:29:09,420 --> 00:29:12,920
The new century was a kaleidoscope of images and ideas,

473
00:29:13,020 --> 00:29:14,820
and in the chaotic American culture,

474
00:29:14,920 --> 00:29:18,620
the Statue of Liberty became a fashion favorite.

475
00:29:18,720 --> 00:29:21,620
Its image appeared on all manner of trinkets.

476
00:29:21,720 --> 00:29:25,220
Even today, early renderings are highly prized.

477
00:29:27,700 --> 00:29:34,740
It's very strange as I collect different statues and you see the faces and the different images and the quality.

478
00:29:35,940 --> 00:29:39,380
You wonder what the craftsmen had in mind.

479
00:29:39,780 --> 00:29:44,300
One of my favorites of the collection is probably this one here.

480
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:49,620
And this is, it's just so fun that they painted it all up.

481
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:54,300
But inside it said, made in occupied Japan.

482
00:29:54,301 --> 00:29:57,140
Perhaps we cherish Liberty because we realize

483
00:29:57,240 --> 00:29:59,440
that she is vulnerable.

484
00:29:59,540 --> 00:30:02,540
As in 1916, when German saboteurs at the height

485
00:30:02,640 --> 00:30:06,680
of World War I managed to blow up a munition storage facility

486
00:30:06,780 --> 00:30:10,320
in New Jersey, first reports claimed dozens of deaths.

487
00:30:10,420 --> 00:30:13,520
The impact was felt 90 miles away.

488
00:30:13,620 --> 00:30:16,080
Police were called to stop looting in the streets of New

489
00:30:16,180 --> 00:30:18,960
York, and the statue lost nearly 100

490
00:30:19,060 --> 00:30:22,220
bolts in her right arm as a result of the blast.

491
00:30:22,221 --> 00:30:26,420
Still, her torch was held high, and when workmen surveyed the damage,

492
00:30:26,520 --> 00:30:29,960
they wondered if something more than copper and iron

493
00:30:30,060 --> 00:30:33,440
was keeping the statue standing tall and proud.

494
00:30:33,540 --> 00:30:37,540
It was the hope of those who gave us this statue

495
00:30:37,640 --> 00:30:41,140
and the hope of the American people in receiving it

496
00:30:41,240 --> 00:30:46,920
that the goddess of liberty and the goddess of peace were the same.

497
00:30:47,020 --> 00:30:50,320
During the Depression, Liberty carried a double load,

498
00:30:50,321 --> 00:30:55,320
holding both her torch and the nation's morale aloft.

499
00:30:55,420 --> 00:30:58,360
But a few short years later, her light would be dimmed

500
00:30:58,460 --> 00:31:02,360
by a second world war, this one more bloody than the first,

501
00:31:02,460 --> 00:31:04,960
as madmen threatened the future of mankind

502
00:31:05,060 --> 00:31:07,980
and liberty held her breath.

503
00:31:08,080 --> 00:31:10,380
Like so many others, she did her part.

504
00:31:10,480 --> 00:31:12,440
Her image reminded the American people

505
00:31:12,540 --> 00:31:15,120
of what was at stake around the world.

506
00:31:15,220 --> 00:31:16,920
When victory was finally declared,

507
00:31:17,020 --> 00:31:19,520
she joined the exuberant celebrations

508
00:31:19,521 --> 00:31:23,760
as a guest of honor in New York's Times Square.

509
00:31:23,860 --> 00:31:26,820
Ironically, the buoyant spirit at the end of war

510
00:31:26,920 --> 00:31:29,560
disguised the fact that liberty as well as the statue

511
00:31:29,660 --> 00:31:31,160
were fragile.

512
00:31:31,260 --> 00:31:33,700
Freedom demands constant vigilance,

513
00:31:33,800 --> 00:31:39,640
and liberty denied is often the catalyst for social eruption.

514
00:31:39,740 --> 00:31:41,680
In the second half of the 20th century,

515
00:31:41,780 --> 00:31:44,880
many groups who felt alienated from America's mainstream

516
00:31:44,980 --> 00:31:47,880
commandeered the statue to make their desperate political

517
00:31:47,881 --> 00:31:50,780
and desperate political statements.

518
00:31:50,880 --> 00:31:54,120
1956, Hungarian patriots occupy the torch

519
00:31:54,220 --> 00:31:56,760
and display their flag of liberation.

520
00:31:56,860 --> 00:32:00,760
1965, a black liberation organization

521
00:32:00,860 --> 00:32:02,800
is caught plotting to blow up the statue.

522
00:32:06,060 --> 00:32:09,240
1980, two protesters demanding freedom

523
00:32:09,340 --> 00:32:11,100
for an imprisoned Black Panther leader

524
00:32:11,200 --> 00:32:16,040
damage the statue as they climb the liberty's exterior plates.

525
00:32:16,041 --> 00:32:18,540
plus the corroding influence of acid rain

526
00:32:18,640 --> 00:32:21,440
took their toll on the statue, showing just how fragile

527
00:32:21,540 --> 00:32:23,140
she actually was.

528
00:32:23,240 --> 00:32:25,180
Like Ellis Island, the Statue of Liberty

529
00:32:25,280 --> 00:32:29,180
was quickly becoming a neglected relic from America's past.

530
00:32:29,280 --> 00:32:31,480
Pieces of her ornate torch were breaking off

531
00:32:31,580 --> 00:32:34,600
and washing ashore in New Jersey.

532
00:32:34,700 --> 00:32:37,360
So it was with a great burst of patriotic zeal

533
00:32:37,460 --> 00:32:40,740
that America took a second look at both the Statue of Liberty

534
00:32:40,840 --> 00:32:43,300
and Ellis Island.

535
00:32:43,301 --> 00:32:49,140
It started when President Reagan asked Lee Iacocca to mount a private sector effort

536
00:32:49,141 --> 00:32:53,520
to restore both the Statue of Liberty and Ellis Island.

537
00:32:54,320 --> 00:32:58,280
First time I came here was a cold November day in 1982 with Mr.

538
00:32:58,281 --> 00:33:00,480
Iacocca, and the place was a wreck.

539
00:33:01,160 --> 00:33:03,720
I wondered what we'd gotten ourselves into.

540
00:33:04,460 --> 00:33:10,280
We felt that the American people would help us if we could figure out how to ask them to help us.

541
00:33:10,281 --> 00:33:14,600
The years have been hard on Ellis Island, and they've been hard on the lady with the

542
00:33:14,601 --> 00:33:17,080
torch across the harbor, too.

543
00:33:17,081 --> 00:33:21,120
Are we going to preserve these monuments, or will they become just ruins of the past,

544
00:33:21,121 --> 00:33:23,640
like the pyramids or the Colosseum?

545
00:33:23,641 --> 00:33:28,200
Are the values these monuments symbolize still alive in America, or are they part of our

546
00:33:28,201 --> 00:33:30,120
past?

547
00:33:30,121 --> 00:33:35,720
The one guideline was that no money would come from the government, and we fulfill that.

548
00:33:35,721 --> 00:33:38,720
We've raised about $420 million.

549
00:33:38,721 --> 00:33:41,420
There's no way of actually knowing how many people

550
00:33:41,421 --> 00:33:44,520
have given to us, but we estimate about 20 million.

551
00:33:44,521 --> 00:33:47,320
School children alone gave us $6 million.

552
00:33:47,321 --> 00:33:52,080
The engineering challenges to fix the statue were enormous.

553
00:33:52,081 --> 00:33:53,680
And it was a race against the clock

554
00:33:53,681 --> 00:33:56,820
to have her ready for the 100th anniversary.

555
00:33:56,821 --> 00:33:59,300
The winter that we put up the exterior scaffolding

556
00:33:59,301 --> 00:34:02,280
on the statue, it was a very, very cold winter,

557
00:34:02,281 --> 00:34:04,880
a lot of winds, very cold.

558
00:34:04,881 --> 00:34:12,680
And these workers had a race on to see who could get the right bars in place so that who could be the first to give her a kiss on the lips.

559
00:34:13,540 --> 00:34:20,860
Inside the statue, the metal had originally been covered with coats of coal tar and paint, all of which had to be removed.

560
00:34:22,440 --> 00:34:28,180
We had removed the original paint on the inside of the statue using liquid nitrogen.

561
00:34:28,181 --> 00:34:32,900
It had around seven layers of paint, and it would just crackle and come apart.

562
00:34:32,901 --> 00:34:35,220
We thought we were going to spend months doing this,

563
00:34:35,300 --> 00:34:37,300
and we wound up doing it about a week and a half.

564
00:34:38,180 --> 00:34:40,940
But the coal tar was an entirely different situation.

565
00:34:41,080 --> 00:34:41,960
We couldn't get that out.

566
00:34:42,380 --> 00:34:46,560
The black sludge couldn't be sandblasted off by conventional means.

567
00:34:46,880 --> 00:34:49,280
That the skin only being a penny thick,

568
00:34:49,400 --> 00:34:52,460
we couldn't afford to have any copper loss.

569
00:34:52,920 --> 00:34:56,180
So we had to find something that was strong enough to take out the coal tar,

570
00:34:56,340 --> 00:34:57,700
but not hurt the copper.

571
00:34:58,060 --> 00:35:00,680
We finally found something very, very sophisticated,

572
00:35:01,240 --> 00:35:02,120
bicarbonate of soda.

573
00:35:02,121 --> 00:35:06,020
More than 40 tons of bicarbonate of soda were used.

574
00:35:06,480 --> 00:35:10,600
Meanwhile, rusted armatures were swelling against the thin copper skin.

575
00:35:11,800 --> 00:35:16,020
And so it started ripping itself apart, and the weakest link was the copper.

576
00:35:16,520 --> 00:35:23,580
And indeed, a number of the connections were completely destroyed through this period of rusting.

577
00:35:23,581 --> 00:35:31,540
This bar represents one of the first handmade samples of the armature bars that are part

578
00:35:31,541 --> 00:35:36,500
of the tertiary structure that holds the copper in place on the statue.

579
00:35:36,501 --> 00:35:41,440
And of course, originally it was very easy to make these because they had already used

580
00:35:41,441 --> 00:35:47,160
a process that is called repoussé, a literal translation is pushing from the reverse side,

581
00:35:47,161 --> 00:35:53,400
in which they made the copper, bent the copper on forms that were reverse forms.

582
00:35:53,401 --> 00:35:56,160
So then they just bent these bars on the same forms,

583
00:35:56,161 --> 00:35:58,540
and the two matched very nicely.

584
00:35:58,541 --> 00:36:00,100
We, of course, had another challenge.

585
00:36:00,101 --> 00:36:01,800
We didn't have the forms, but we

586
00:36:01,801 --> 00:36:06,380
had to replace over 1,800 bars, no two of which were the same.

587
00:36:06,381 --> 00:36:10,380
It was exhausting hand-wrought repoussé work,

588
00:36:10,381 --> 00:36:13,200
which 100 years after the original construction

589
00:36:13,201 --> 00:36:15,060
was becoming a lost art.

590
00:36:17,980 --> 00:36:23,400
During the two world wars, in 1914 to 1918 and in 1945,

591
00:36:23,401 --> 00:36:26,480
We lost many skilled people trained in this technique.

592
00:36:28,340 --> 00:36:30,880
So today it's a technique like a lot of the arts,

593
00:36:31,100 --> 00:36:34,580
which has lacked the craftsmen due to war casualties.

594
00:36:35,740 --> 00:36:38,920
French artisans were the only workers in the world

595
00:36:38,921 --> 00:36:41,060
who still performed repoussé,

596
00:36:41,660 --> 00:36:43,060
and their numbers were dwindling.

597
00:36:43,740 --> 00:36:46,320
Yet when they came to America to help in the restoration,

598
00:36:46,700 --> 00:36:48,720
they weren't welcomed with open arms.

599
00:36:51,120 --> 00:36:52,200
There was a conflict.

600
00:36:52,201 --> 00:36:53,640
as soon as we arrived.

601
00:36:54,640 --> 00:36:57,040
In France, when we do sculpture,

602
00:36:57,540 --> 00:37:00,880
we can work freely with, for example,

603
00:37:00,881 --> 00:37:03,560
with wood, with plaster, with steel,

604
00:37:04,320 --> 00:37:07,160
without necessarily a trade union or guild.

605
00:37:08,520 --> 00:37:11,520
In your country, there were unions

606
00:37:11,521 --> 00:37:15,320
who didn't understand why parts of wood or plaster

607
00:37:15,321 --> 00:37:17,920
had to be made for them by others.

608
00:37:19,520 --> 00:37:21,540
Fortunately, it was a conflict

609
00:37:21,541 --> 00:37:23,640
that was short-lived.

610
00:37:23,740 --> 00:37:28,040
We had the Americans join the French

611
00:37:28,140 --> 00:37:28,840
and come over here.

612
00:37:28,940 --> 00:37:30,540
And when they came over here, they taught

613
00:37:30,640 --> 00:37:34,680
our American metal workers how to do this reposé work.

614
00:37:34,780 --> 00:37:38,420
So basically, the respect that they had for one another

615
00:37:38,520 --> 00:37:39,700
and the respect that the Americans had

616
00:37:39,800 --> 00:37:43,760
for the French technique wound up being what made them

617
00:37:43,860 --> 00:37:44,660
all workable.

618
00:37:44,760 --> 00:37:46,760
They became very close friends in most cases.

619
00:37:48,700 --> 00:37:51,300
Today, 10 years later, we take

620
00:37:51,301 --> 00:37:54,400
into our studios, who come to study for about two to three

621
00:37:54,500 --> 00:37:58,780
weeks, to whom we explain the repoussé process.

622
00:37:58,880 --> 00:38:02,340
Reclaiming the original look and attitude of the statue

623
00:38:02,440 --> 00:38:05,780
was a primary goal of the restoration process,

624
00:38:05,880 --> 00:38:09,520
particularly when the work focused on Liberty's flame.

625
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:12,220
Bartholdi's original design was for the flame

626
00:38:12,320 --> 00:38:15,400
to be a solid object covered in gold

627
00:38:15,500 --> 00:38:17,860
and reflecting exterior light.

628
00:38:17,960 --> 00:38:19,820
But through the years, crude attempts

629
00:38:19,821 --> 00:38:23,200
attempts were made to carve out panels and allow light to stream

630
00:38:23,201 --> 00:38:27,400
from the inside through colored glass.

631
00:38:27,401 --> 00:38:30,260
Gutsam Borglum, the man who carved Mount Rushmore,

632
00:38:30,261 --> 00:38:34,260
had made the greatest design change and the greatest error.

633
00:38:34,261 --> 00:38:36,700
His awkward recrafting of the flame

634
00:38:36,701 --> 00:38:39,340
kept the statue from being watertight.

635
00:38:39,341 --> 00:38:42,380
Yeah, that was the source of a lot of the water,

636
00:38:42,381 --> 00:38:46,720
with the salt air and the water running down on the inside

637
00:38:46,721 --> 00:38:51,160
of the copper and coming in contact with the iron armature

638
00:38:51,260 --> 00:38:54,420
that was the original structure within the statue.

639
00:38:54,520 --> 00:38:58,060
Today's new flame is Bartholdi's old idea,

640
00:38:58,160 --> 00:39:02,140
a solid, gilded crescendo, simple and pure.

641
00:39:02,240 --> 00:39:06,080
Liberty is again enlightening the world.

642
00:39:06,180 --> 00:39:10,720
On July 4, 1986, the party began.

643
00:39:10,820 --> 00:39:12,820
It was called Liberty Weekend, and it

644
00:39:12,920 --> 00:39:16,720
was shared with a billion and a half people around the world.

645
00:39:16,721 --> 00:39:21,720
We are the keepers of the flame of liberty.

646
00:39:21,721 --> 00:39:26,720
I ask that you all join me in this symbolic act of faith,

647
00:39:26,721 --> 00:39:29,720
this lighting of Miss Liberty's torch.

648
00:39:36,720 --> 00:39:40,720
As the celebration exploded across the night sky,

649
00:39:40,721 --> 00:39:43,720
it sparked a new spirit of awe and wonder

650
00:39:43,721 --> 00:39:45,720
at the meaning of liberty.

651
00:39:54,860 --> 00:39:57,560
100 years ago, when the world dreamt of freedom,

652
00:39:57,660 --> 00:40:00,700
the image of a lady with a torch came to mind.

653
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:02,700
Today, when people long for liberty,

654
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:05,840
the very same image captures their imagination.

655
00:40:08,780 --> 00:40:10,220
Beijing, China.

656
00:40:10,320 --> 00:40:12,520
Students demonstrate for democracy,

657
00:40:12,521 --> 00:40:15,860
sculpting a statue to symbolize their hopes and dreams.

658
00:40:18,260 --> 00:40:21,060
Here, the spirit of liberty still resonates.

659
00:40:21,160 --> 00:40:24,400
Standing up against tyranny, these students reminded us

660
00:40:24,500 --> 00:40:27,560
that all are created equal.

661
00:40:27,660 --> 00:40:31,440
Half a world away, the national effort to restore Ellis Island

662
00:40:31,540 --> 00:40:35,880
and the Statue of Liberty reflects the very same ideal,

663
00:40:35,980 --> 00:40:39,320
blending a celebration of the great American melting pot

664
00:40:39,321 --> 00:40:43,060
with a bittersweet recollection of our immigrant past.

665
00:40:45,760 --> 00:40:50,860
The statue has always been the symbol of America.

666
00:40:50,960 --> 00:40:54,060
And Ellis Island has always been the story of America.

667
00:40:54,160 --> 00:40:58,080
So you see, both of them are important in different ways.

668
00:40:58,180 --> 00:41:01,840
Any American that comes down and sees Ellis Island

669
00:41:01,940 --> 00:41:04,580
or Statue of Liberty should walk tall,

670
00:41:04,680 --> 00:41:06,080
and they should walk proud.

671
00:41:06,180 --> 00:41:08,480
It was a great thing that they accomplished.

672
00:41:08,481 --> 00:41:12,580
It is rare and inspiring that so much energy and time

673
00:41:12,680 --> 00:41:16,580
and money has been committed by so many common individuals

674
00:41:16,680 --> 00:41:19,660
to create a monument that doesn't commemorate a great battle

675
00:41:19,760 --> 00:41:21,760
or a popular leader.

676
00:41:21,860 --> 00:41:25,600
Instead, the Statue of Liberty celebrates an idea,

677
00:41:25,700 --> 00:41:28,700
that elusive goal of universal freedom,

678
00:41:28,800 --> 00:41:31,140
which has never been totally achieved.

679
00:41:31,240 --> 00:41:34,040
This is the vision to which America runs,

680
00:41:34,140 --> 00:41:35,880
sometimes stumbling and sometimes

681
00:41:35,881 --> 00:41:39,680
sometimes carrying other nations along with it.

682
00:41:39,780 --> 00:41:43,320
Liberty is not carved in the marble hallways of the past.

683
00:41:43,420 --> 00:41:45,760
It is our dream of the future.

684
00:41:45,860 --> 00:41:49,220
America laughs and cries and loves and bleeds

685
00:41:49,320 --> 00:41:50,500
in the quest for freedom.

686
00:41:53,600 --> 00:41:56,300
Here at the Statue of Liberty and Ellis Island,

687
00:41:56,400 --> 00:41:59,400
that idea takes a solid form.

688
00:41:59,500 --> 00:42:03,080
But always behind these symbols remains the quest,

689
00:42:03,081 --> 00:42:07,520
freedom yet to be attained, liberty preserved.

690
00:42:07,620 --> 00:42:15,420
Oh, beautiful for patriot dream

691
00:42:15,520 --> 00:42:22,540
that sees beyond the years,

692
00:42:22,640 --> 00:42:30,140
thine alabaster cities gleam,

693
00:42:30,141 --> 00:42:35,900
Undimmed by human tears

694
00:42:36,900 --> 00:42:44,380
America, America

695
00:42:44,780 --> 00:42:50,900
God shed his grace on thee

696
00:42:52,100 --> 00:42:59,940
And crowned thy good with brotherhood

697
00:43:00,140 --> 00:43:14,140
From sea to shining sea.

